{"choices":[{"finish_reason":"stop","index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"What is there to say about the ballet? — a subject that no one has yet thought to treat in a ballet, and which would hardly occur to anyone else — namely, a sleepwalker. In the first act, there is dancing — with all its complications; yet there is a plot. Grace, cheerfulness, agility (the corps de ballet has it too!); in the second act, a sleepwalker appears, coming through the window on a ladder with a lantern in her hand, into the master of the house’s bedroom, lies down — after having knelt and prayed (the French around me said she was a Protestant, since it is not a church in which she prays; Protestant churches are officially called temples here). — The master — out of reverence — climbs out of the same window and leaves her alone — great applause for this virtuous behavior. The third act begins with the greatest anger of the sleepwalker’s fiancé, that she was found sleeping in a gentleman’s room. The gentleman explains to him and to the entire audience that she came into his room as a sleepwalker; they do not want to comprehend or understand it; then she appears once more on the roof, strolling with the lantern and marching down from a high, dangerously collapsed wall; — reconciliation ends everything. — In the last two acts, there is no more dancing — but much grace and liveliness in the pantomime, which admittedly cannot be understandable everywhere. — Yesterday I was in St. Cloud — beautiful surroundings — on the bank of the Seine, whose curves here form almost a complete circle, enclosing the vineyard hill; before one lies Paris with its beautiful towers and domes and countless houses. —"}}],"created":1795080571,"model":"qwen","system_fingerprint":"b11429-d81235049","object":"chat.completion","usage":{"completion_tokens":346,"prompt_tokens":483,"total_tokens":829,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0}},"id":"chatcmpl-U1k7TDAMcsgDxNAsx2ZvdihvN2QwWQYT","timings":{"cac